WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.070
Have you ever wondered why atoms stick

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:05.039
together to form molecules?

00:00:05.039 --> 00:00:06.861
It's all about stability!

00:00:06.861 --> 00:00:09.777
Atoms want to have a full outer shell of

00:00:09.777 --> 00:00:12.475
electrons, just like the noble gases.

00:00:12.475 --> 00:00:15.464
This makes them much more stable and less

00:00:15.464 --> 00:00:16.120
reactive.

00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:19.579
Many atoms, especially non-metals,

00:00:19.579 --> 00:00:22.240
can achieve this stability by sharing

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.182
electrons with other atoms.

00:00:24.182 --> 00:00:25.549
In a covalent bond,

00:00:25.549 --> 00:00:27.779
atoms share pairs of electrons.

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:30.296
This is different from transferring

00:00:30.296 --> 00:00:33.173
electrons, which happens in ionic bonds.

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:37.113
Let's look at hydrogen chloride, HCl.

00:00:37.113 --> 00:00:40.457
The hydrogen atom shares one electron with

00:00:40.457 --> 00:00:41.890
the chlorine atom,

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:44.199
and chlorine shares one back.

00:00:44.199 --> 00:00:46.189
This forms a shared pair.

00:00:46.189 --> 00:00:49.454
This sharing lets each atom reach a full,

00:00:49.454 --> 00:00:50.967
stable outer shell.

00:00:51.967 --> 00:00:52.952
For hydrogen,

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:55.907
a full outer shell means two electrons.

00:00:55.907 --> 00:00:58.863
For chlorine, it means eight electrons,

00:00:58.863 --> 00:01:00.605
also known as an octet.

00:01:00.605 --> 00:01:03.788
By sharing, both atoms get what they need.

00:01:03.788 --> 00:01:06.668
This is the main reason covalent bonds

00:01:06.668 --> 00:01:07.047
form.

00:01:08.033 --> 00:01:10.667
The shared pair of electrons isn't just

00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:12.490
floating between the atoms.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:15.258
It's strongly attracted to the positively

00:01:15.258 --> 00:01:17.217
charged nuclei of both atoms.

00:01:17.217 --> 00:01:19.918
This mutual attraction is what holds the

00:01:19.918 --> 00:01:22.213
atoms together in a covalent bond.

00:01:23.233 --> 00:01:26.128
Covalent bonds involve sharing electron

00:01:26.128 --> 00:01:29.023
pairs, usually between non-metal atoms.

00:01:29.023 --> 00:01:31.843
This sharing helps each atom achieve a

00:01:31.843 --> 00:01:33.698
stable, full outer shell.

00:01:33.698 --> 00:01:36.816
The shared electrons are attracted to both

00:01:36.816 --> 00:01:39.413
nuclei, holding the atoms together.
